The investigation is finished. The report is at `collab/1791201600000-engineer-python-test-cost-investigation.findings.md`, and the test-side prototype is left uncommitted in 5 test files (+34/−11). There were no git writes, I never ran the full suite, and every run passed with the same pass counts before and after.

**Why every Python test costs seconds**
- **The prelude is not re-lowered per test.** `prelude/uast` is built once when the namespace loads. Per test, marking tail calls on the whole tree and converting it to datoms take only 1-3 ms.
- **~99% of each run is compiling and admitting one whole image on each VM.** Each image is the prelude plus the program together. The tests can't cache the prelude part, because each VM compiles that whole image as one unit.
- **Per-run floor:** a parity run costs 1.44 s on the JVM, 2.99 s on Node and 10.8 s on Dart. The register VM is 60% of that on the JVM and 83% on Dart.

**What is slow on Dart:** two product functions account for about 7-8 s of the 10.8 s.
- **`dao.jing/sha256`:** the Dart version is a hand-written SHA-256. It takes 3.7-5.0 s to hash the 2.2 MB register image. `package:crypto`, already a dependency in `pubspec.yaml`, takes 27 ms and gives the same digest.
- **`body-liveness`:** for each of 835 bodies it allocates a vector as long as the whole image (11,304 slots), so cost grows with the square of prelude size. It runs twice per register run, about 1.4 s each time on Dart.

The three Dart outliers have no problem of their own. Each is just its number of runs times the Dart floor: `set-recursion-limit-test` is 10 hooked runs at ~13-15 s each.

**Test-side prototype**
- **What it does:** stack and register now share one `resolve` per run instead of resolving twice. `adapt` is defined as exactly that composition. Separately, `derived-ast` in the safepoint tests is memoized.
- **One dropped piece:** I first memoized `derive*` too. That would have made `insertion-is-deterministic-test` compare a cached value with itself, so I removed it.
- **Saving:** about 6% of the JVM fast form and 8% on Node (parity: 14.4 s → 13.3 s). Multi-run slow tests gain more; `set-recursion-limit-test` went from 18.6 s to 15.3 s.
- **Measurement caveat:** load average reached 29, so the JVM numbers rely on call counts and CPU time rather than wall time.
- **Checks:** Dart compiles and passes the safepoint fast form. kondo is clean. I did not run cljstyle; four changed lines are over 80 columns, so run `cljstyle fix` before landing.

**Recommendation**
1. **Land now:** the test-side change, after cljstyle. It is small and safe.
2. **Schedule next:** small product fixes, none in the engine or ucf directories:
   - Use `package:crypto` for SHA-256 on Dart: about −5.4 s per Dart run.
   - Give `body-liveness` per-body vectors: prototyped on the JVM with identical output and hashes, −0.2 s per run; on Dart I estimate about −2.9 s per run.
   - Rewrite `to-hex` without per-digit string building: −0.1 s per JVM run.
   - Stop rebuilding the AST walker's 45-field record through the positional constructor on every step: this is about 66% of walker time on the JVM.

   With the test-side change I estimate the floor drops to ~1.0 s on the JVM and ~2-2.5 s on Dart. The Python fast form would then be roughly JVM ~90 s and Dart ~30-35 s, down from ~120 s and ~100 s.
3. **Later:** the linked prelude described in `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.6 (prerequisites L-a, L-b and P2) is the real fix for the floor. It also needs a cache of admitted images keyed by identity, because `attach-image` hashes the combined image again.

I don't recommend running fewer VMs in the fast lane now: after these fixes the register VM is much cheaper, and it is where cross-host bugs show up. All the "after" totals are estimates from per-phase costs; only the test-side change was measured end to end.

The Gmail, Google Calendar and Google Drive connectors need to be authorized in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. They weren't needed for this task.
